/-!
## The norm theorem

The key computation is formula (65): the operator $`s=\tau_Q\sigma` acts on divisors with
$`\deg_q(sw)=\deg_q w` and $`\Phi_q(sw)=\sigma\Phi_q(w)\cdot Q^{\deg w}`.
-/

#stmt degQ_PhiQ_divAct

/-!
Every element of $`K^\times` is the norm of a $`q`-periodic function; it is the quotient of two
theta functions.
-/

#stmt exists_norm_const

/-!
*Theorem XVI (norm theorem).* _Every nonzero semi-periodic function is the norm
$`\prod_{i<n}(\tau_Q\sigma)^i g` of a function $`g\in F_L(q)`; equivalently
$`\hat H^0(G_{\mathbf Q},F_L(q)^\times)=0`._
-/

#stmt thmXVI_norm

/-!
## The cohomology table

Tate cohomology of the cyclic group generated by $`s=\tau_Q\sigma` is computed concretely
({name}`AnalyticTheory.cycH0`, {name}`AnalyticTheory.cycH1`). The rows are: the periodic divisors
$`D_L(q)` (both groups vanish, since $`D_L(q)` is a free permutation module), the degree zero
divisors $`D^0_L(q)` ($`\hat H^0=0`, $`\hat H^1\cong\mathbb Z/n`), the classes
$`C^0_L(q)\cong L^\times/q` ($`\hat H^0\cong K^\times/NL^\times`, $`\hat H^1\cong\mathbb Z/n`), the
principal divisors $`P_L(q)` ($`\hat H^0=0`, $`\hat H^1\cong K^\times/NL^\times`) and
$`F_L(q)^\times` (both groups vanish: $`\hat H^0` by Theorem XVI, $`\hat H^1` by Hilbert's
Theorem 90 for the cyclic extension $`F_L(q)\,|\,F_K(\mathbf Q)`).

(Correction: the book's $`\hat H^1(D^0_L(q))\cong\mathbb Z/n` is stated here with `ZMod [L:K]`.)

Hilbert's Theorem 90 is reproved for fields of arbitrary universe, and for an arbitrary
automorphism of finite order:
-/

